<%!-- Unlike the media kit beside it, this page follows the reader's language
      (see VutuvWeb.CompanyController), so it carries no `lang` of its own and
      every sentence of its argument comes from VutuvWeb.AgentDocs.InvestorsDoc
      — the one chokepoint the four agent formats render as well. --%>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl space-y-6 py-6">
  <.card>
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
      {InvestorsDoc.headline()}
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-lg text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      {InvestorsDoc.purpose()}
    </p>
  </.card>

  <%!-- The figures come before the argument: they are the one thing on this
        page nobody has to take our word for, and a reader who does not believe
        the numbers will not read the reasoning either. --%>
  <.card>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">{gettext("Where we are")}</h2>

    <%!-- Two figures and the reading of them, in one row: the tiles sit side by
          side at every width (two short figures need no full line each, not
          even on a phone), and from `lg` up the explanation takes a third
          column beside them instead of a paragraph underneath. Below that it
          spans both tiles, which is where it was. --%>
    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 lg:grid-cols-3">
      <.figure_tile label={gettext("Members")} value={delimited_count(@facts.members)} />
      <.figure_tile
        label={gettext("Fediverse accounts")}
        value={delimited_count(@facts.fediverse_accounts)}
        note={
          ngettext(
            "From %{formatted} server",
            "From %{formatted} servers",
            @facts.fediverse_servers,
            formatted: delimited_count(@facts.fediverse_servers)
          )
        }
      />
      <div class="col-span-2 min-w-0 lg:col-span-1">
        <%!-- The arithmetic behind the figure in the top bar, beside the two
              tiles it adds: investors have asked how that number comes about,
              and the answer is an addition whose summands are right there.
              `tabular-nums` so the digits line up as an equation rather than
              as prose. --%>
        <p class="tabular-nums font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          {InvestorsDoc.people_sum(@facts)}
        </p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
          {InvestorsDoc.counter_explainer()}
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <.growth_curve series={@facts.series} class="mt-6" height="h-40 lg:h-56" />
    <p :if={@growth_sentence} class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
      {@growth_sentence}
    </p>
  </.card>

  <.card>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
      {gettext("Why this is worth building")}
    </h2>
    <%!-- Four claims, so a 2x2 grid from `lg` up rather than a column of
          full-width paragraphs: at this card's width a line of prose runs past
          a comfortable measure, and the tiles halve it while giving each claim
          a surface of its own, the same one the figures above sit on. The
          citation is pinned to the bottom of its tile (`mt-auto`) so the four
          of them line up across a row whatever the text above them does. --%>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div
        :for={point <- InvestorsDoc.case_points()}
        class="flex flex-col rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 dark:bg-slate-800/60"
      >
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">{point.title}</h3>
        <p class="mt-1 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">{point.body}</p>
        <%!-- A claim that leans on somebody else's measurement names it and
              links it: an investor checks a figure like this, and one they
              cannot check reads as one we made up. --%>
        <p :if={point.source} class="mt-auto pt-3 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
          {gettext("Source:")}
          <a
            href={point.source.url}
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            target="_blank"
            class="font-semibold text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 dark:hover:text-brand-300"
          >{point.source.label}</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </.card>

  <%!-- No email address: an investor writes through vutuv, which keeps a
        personal inbox off a page written to be read by strangers and machines.
        The profile is linked, because somebody about to put six figures
        somewhere wants to see who is on the other side first. Rendered only
        where the configured handle really resolves to a member here, so a
        third-party installation offers its own operator or stays silent. --%>
  <.card :if={@contact_handle}>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">{gettext("Write to me")}</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      {InvestorsDoc.contact_note()}
    </p>
    <p :if={@contact_profile_url} class="mt-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      {gettext("My profile:")}
      <a
        href={@contact_profile_url}
        class="font-semibold break-all text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 dark:hover:text-brand-300"
      >{@contact_profile_url}</a>
    </p>
    <%!-- Which of the two leads depends on who is reading. A message needs a
          session, so for a logged-out reader "Send a message" is a redirect to
          the login form and a dead end for somebody who has no account yet:
          they are offered the account first. A member gets the reverse, since
          for them the message is one click and the other button is nothing. --%>
    <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <.button :if={is_nil(@current_user)} href={~p"/"}>{gettext("Create an account")}</.button>
      <.button
        href={~p"/messages/with/#{@contact_handle}"}
        variant={if @current_user, do: "primary", else: "secondary"}
      >
        {gettext("Send a message")}
      </.button>
    </div>
  </.card>
</div>
